# AMD64 thunk export spec -- msvcrt.dll.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GUEST ARGUMENT REGISTERS AT THE TRAP  (load-bearing ABI -- read this)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# x86-64 SYSCALL architecturally writes the address of the following
# instruction into RCX and RFLAGS into R11.  RCX is MS-x64's FIRST argument
# register, so a bare `0f 05` stub destroys arg0 before the host can see it.
# (Measured: a trap in GetModuleHandleW arrived with rcx = RIP+2.)  Each trap
# stub therefore rescues arg0 first, the same trick the Linux syscall ABI uses
# for its own 4th argument:
#
#       49 89 ca    mov r10, rcx      ; arg0 out of the way
#       0f 05       syscall           ; free to clobber rcx/r11
#       cc ...      int3 padding to the 16-byte stride
#
# So the host-visible argument registers are:
#
#       arg0 -> R10   (rescued from RCX -- NOT RCX)
#       arg1 -> RDX
#       arg2 -> R8
#       arg3 -> R9
#       arg4+ -> [RSP + 8 + i*8]   (past the pushed return address and the
#                                   32-byte shadow space)
#
# The trapping RIP is  stubs_rva + index*stride + trap_off  (trap_off = 3 in
# trap mode, 0 in direct mode), published in __wine_thunk_info -- do not infer
# it from the stub bytes.
#
# Every export here is __cdecl, not __stdcall.  That changes nothing above:
# MS-x64 has a single calling convention and the decoration is inert on this
# target.  It does mean the CALLER cleans the stack, so a wrong nargs is not
# self-correcting the way a stdcall mistake would be -- it just corrupts the
# guest's stack.  Hence the same rule as kernel32.thunks and ucrtbase.thunks:
# the columns are assertions checked against Wine's headers, never hand-tuned
# to pass.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ORDER IS ABI ONCE USED.  This file is new as of this commit, so the order
# below was chosen freely (descending corpus demand, with the two .spec-sourced
# entries sorted to the end).  From the first time the host-side dispatcher
# consumes it, the order below IS the stub index order and is part of the ABI:
# APPEND ONLY, NEVER REORDER, NEVER DELETE A LINE.
#
# Columns:
#   <ExportName>  <imm32 returned by --body=direct>  <nargs>  <int|void>  <header>:<line>
#   <ExportName>  <imm32>  <nfixed>  <int|void>  <header>:<line>  variadic=<v-variant>
#
# The sixth column is optional and is the ONLY way to describe a true `...`
# export; five columns mean exactly what they always meant.  For a variadic the
# <nargs> column is the number of FIXED arguments -- where the `...` begins.
#
# The last three columns are ASSERTIONS, not inputs.  gen_thunk_pe.py re-reads
# each declaration out of Wine's own headers with clang and hard-fails if this
# file disagrees.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THESE TWENTY, AND WHERE THE CLIFF IS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Computed over guest-corpus/imports (447 binaries, *surface* lists excluded;
# 339 of them import msvcrt.dll, 354 distinct msvcrt symbols demanded):
#
#     339  strrchr strlen strcmp _vsnprintf _strdup __iob_func
#     338  strncmp exit atoi _onexit _initterm __set_app_type __getmainargs
#     337  printf
#     336  vprintf setvbuf fflush _isatty _get_osfhandle _fileno
#     335  puts
#     ---- CLIFF: 335 -> 258, a 77-binary drop ----
#     258  strcpy
#     255  free
#     251  strchr
#     249  getenv fwrite
#     ...
#
# The >=300 cut is not a judgement call here: the distribution is bimodal.
# Twenty-one symbols sit in a 335..339 band (they are the mingw/MSVC CRT
# startup-and-console preamble that essentially every corpus binary links),
# then nothing at all until 258.  Any threshold anywhere in 259..335 selects
# exactly the same twenty-one names.
#
# All twenty-one are below: the twenty at indices 0..19, and printf, which was
# refused when this file was written and is now emitted at index 20 -- see the
# variadic banner immediately following.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VARIADIC EXPORTS (index 20 onwards).  THIS SUPERSEDES THE OLD "printf IS
# REFUSED" BANNER; THE REASONING THAT PRODUCED THAT REFUSAL IS RECORDED HERE SO
# NOBODY REINSTATES IT.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#     @ varargs printf(str)                     dlls/msvcrt/msvcrt.spec:1398
#     __WINE_CRT_PRINTF_ATTR(1,2) int printf(const char *, ...)
#                                               include/msvcrt/stdio.h:701
#
# The old objection was that a fixed-arity stub cannot forward `...`, because
# it cannot learn how many arguments the caller pushed.  That much is true and
# still is.  The error was concluding that the stub must therefore CALL the
# variadic function.  It does not.  The host calls that function's own
# v-variant --
#
#       printf     -> vprintf         (1 fixed arg  -> 2 args)
#       _snprintf  -> _vsnprintf      (3 fixed args -> 4 args)
#
# -- handing it a va_list synthesised from the guest's own argument frame
# starting at slot <nfixed>.  No argument count is ever needed: the v-variant
# walks the list under the format string's control, exactly as it does for a
# native caller.
#
# WHY THAT IS SOUND HERE AND WOULD NOT BE ELSEWHERE.  MS-x64 homes RCX/RDX/R8/R9
# into the caller's 32-byte shadow space, so from the first vararg onward the
# guest's arguments are a flat run of 8-byte slots and a pointer to the first
# of them IS an MS-x64 va_list.  ppc64le ELFv2 va_list is likewise a plain
# pointer into a flat 8-byte-slot parameter save area -- there is no
# register-save-area half to reconstruct, unlike SysV x86-64, where this would
# require rebuilding a __va_list_tag and would not work.  MEASURED end to end on
# the AC922: a guest-built MS-x64 va_list consumed correctly by native ppc64
# _vsnprintf, "%d-%s-%d" -> "4242-AB-77".
#
# The sibling project ~/Development/powerpc64le-ports/wine-spec-thunk refuses
# va_list outright.  That refusal was written for a different boundary and is
# not an ABI fact about this one.  Do not copy it back.
#
# THE SIXTH COLUMN, variadic=<impl>, IS AN ASSERTION LIKE EVERY OTHER COLUMN,
# and spec2thunk checks it in both directions before emitting anything:
#   * a variadic WITHOUT variadic= is still refused, exactly as before;
#   * variadic= on a function clang says is NOT variadic is a hard error;
#   * the named v-variant is resolved by the oracle and must take exactly
#     nfixed+1 arguments (va_list is one ordinary pointer parameter to it).
# The v-variant name is never derived by munging the export name.  It is
# carried to the host in impl_names_rva[], new in __wine_thunk_info version 4.
#
# WHAT IT UNLOCKS.  Counted, not estimated: of the 339 corpus binaries that
# import msvcrt, the number whose ENTIRE msvcrt import list is satisfied by
# indices 0..19 alone is ZERO -- printf alone blocked 337 of them.  With printf
# it is 45.  Adding the next tier (strcpy, free, strchr, getenv, fwrite,
# strcspn, memmove, wcslen -- the 207..258 band just under the cliff) makes it
# 92; adding the 82..154 band (memset, memcmp, memcpy, malloc, wcscmp, calloc)
# makes it 166.
#
# NOTE THE OLD ASYMMETRY, WHICH WAS NEVER AN INCONSISTENCY: _vsnprintf and
# vprintf are emitted at indices 3 and 11 as ORDINARY exports.  They are not
# variadic -- the caller has already built the va_list and passes it as one
# pointer -- so they carry no variadic= column and their descriptors have bit 9
# clear.  They are also the impl targets of indices 20 and 21, which is why
# those two v-variants must stay in this file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KNOWN LATENT HAZARD -- host->guest callback.  READ BEFORE DEBUGGING A CRASH
# HERE.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Three entries below traffic in GUEST function pointers that something later
# CALLS.  We have no host->guest call path, so the native ppc64 implementation
# would branch into x86-64 bytes and die.  They are emitted anyway because in
# the common case nothing is ever registered and all three are no-ops:
#
#   _initterm(_PVFV *first, _PVFV *last)   -- walks an array of guest function
#       pointers and calls each non-NULL entry IMMEDIATELY.  Harmless when the
#       initialiser array is empty (first == last), which is the usual case for
#       a C program with no static constructors.  A guest with real C++ static
#       constructors WILL crash here.
#
#   _onexit(_onexit_t func)                -- STORES a guest function pointer;
#       the call is deferred to exit() time.  Registration itself is safe; the
#       bomb goes off later.
#
#   exit(int)                              -- and this is where it goes off.
#       msvcrt's exit() runs the registered atexit/_onexit table.  If _onexit
#       above ever succeeded in recording a guest pointer, exit() calls it.
#       exit() is safe only for as long as that table stays empty.
#
# The descriptors for all three are CORRECT.  The missing piece is the callback
# path, not the arity -- do not "fix" this by editing the columns.
#
# SINCE THE ABOVE WAS WRITTEN there IS a host->guest call path, so the three
# entries described above are no longer latent: _initterm walks the caller's
# table through a form that wraps each entry as it is reached, and _onexit and
# exit are rows in the registration-interception table in
# dlls/ntdll/signal_ppc64.c.  The paragraphs are kept rather than deleted
# because they still name exactly which entries traffic in guest pointers and
# why, which is what someone debugging a crash here needs; only the "we have
# no host->guest call path" premise has expired.
#
# Three more of this module's exports are rows in that same table, added when
# msvcr100's copies of them were: _beginthread and _beginthreadex, whose start
# routines reach native code through msvcrt's OWN native trampoline (see
# dlls/msvcrt/thread.c) rather than through the thread-start interception, and
# __setusermatherr, whose handler native math_error calls and whose int return
# it tests.  This module exports neither _set_invalid_parameter_handler nor
# _set_purecall_handler -- it has _invalid_parameter and _purecall, which are
# the CALL sites and not registrations -- so it has no row for either and
# needs none; msvcr100 and ucrtbase, which do export them, carry those rows.
# See the banner in dlls/msvcr100/msvcr100.thunks for the arity and return
# width each one required and why.
#
# __getmainargs was checked for the same shape and DOES NOT HAVE IT.  Its
# signature (dlls/msvcrt/data.c:497) is
#     int CDECL __getmainargs(int *argc, char ***argv, char ***envp,
#                             int expand_wildcards, _startupinfo *new_mode)
# -- five pointer/int arguments, no function pointer anywhere.  It only WRITES
# out-parameters, and guest and host share one address space, so the argv/envp
# vectors it allocates need no marshalling.  It is ordinary.
#
DLL msvcrt.dll

# Export list from msvcrt.spec; the lines below become overrides (this is
# where the variadic= opt-ins live).  See kernel32.thunks.
FROM-SPEC auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# indices 0..17: the core, in descending corpus demand.  Every entry in this
# file -- these eighteen and the two at 18..19 -- is clang-verified against
# Wine's include/msvcrt/, return kind included; nothing here uses the .spec
# arity fallback.  The dlls/msvcrt/msvcrt.spec lines in the trailing comments
# are an independent corroboration of the arity only; the header is the source
# of record.
#
# The oracle reads these headers in MSVCRT's CRT mode (-D_MSVCR_VER=0), which
# is not the default -- see the banner at indices 18..19 before touching any
# header:line below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# indices 0..5: demanded by 339 of 339 msvcrt-importing binaries.
strrchr             0x00000C01  2  int   string.h:65     # spec:1451 (str long)
# strlen/strncmp return/take size_t, which is 64-bit here and fits one slot.
strlen              0x00000C02  1  int   string.h:57     # spec:1443 (str)
strcmp              0x00000C03  2  int   string.h:51     # spec:1435 (str str)
# _vsnprintf(char *, size_t, const char *, va_list) -- FOUR ordinary slots.
# NOT a variadic: the caller built the va_list.  See the printf banner above.
_vsnprintf          0x00000C04  4  int   stdio.h:720     # spec:1072 (ptr long str ptr)
# _strdup carries __WINE_MALLOC, which clang prints as a MacroQualifiedType and
# which does not affect the declared shape.  Same situation as kernel32's
# HeapAlloc.  The returned buffer is host heap that the guest frees with
# msvcrt's own free() -- not yet in this file, see the append list.
_strdup             0x00000C05  1  int   string.h:26     # spec:960 (str)
# __iob_func() -> &__iob[0], the base of the real FILE array.  The header
# declares it only in the non-__i386__ branch (stdio.h:66, inside
# `#ifndef _STDIO_DEFINED / # ifdef __i386__ ... # else`); our target is
# x86_64-windows, so that IS the branch that applies.  The FILE* it returns is
# HOST memory the guest only ever hands back to other CRT exports (_fileno,
# fflush, setvbuf); nothing dereferences it guest-side.
__iob_func          0x00000C06  0  int   stdio.h:66      # spec:219 ()

# indices 6..10: demanded by 338.  (__set_app_type and __getmainargs are also
# 338 but have no header declaration; they sort to the end.)
strncmp             0x00000C07  3  int   string.h:60     # spec:1446 (str str long)
# exit is declared noreturn.  The descriptor has no bit for that and does not
# need one -- the host implementation simply never returns to the dispatcher.
# HAZARD: exit runs the _onexit/atexit table.  See the banner above.
exit                0x00000C08  1  void  stdlib.h:223    # spec:1286 (long)
atoi                0x00000C09  1  int   stdlib.h:214    # spec:1266 (str)
# HAZARD: stores a GUEST function pointer.  See the banner above.
_onexit             0x00000C0A  1  int   stdlib.h:186    # spec:847 (ptr)
# HAZARD: CALLS guest function pointers immediately.  See the banner above.
_initterm           0x00000C0B  2  void  corecrt_startup.h:29  # spec:561 (ptr ptr)

# indices 11..16: demanded by 336.
# vprintf is stdio.h:711, `_ACRTIMP int __cdecl vprintf(const char*,va_list)`
# -- the real msvcrt import declaration.  stdio.h ALSO carries a UCRT-only
# `static inline` vprintf at :409 that forwards to __stdio_common_vfprintf;
# that one is not this DLL's export and is the wrong branch for us.  Probing
# in the correct CRT mode is what keeps them apart -- see the banner at indices
# 18..19.  Like _vsnprintf, NOT a variadic: the caller built the va_list.
vprintf             0x00000C0C  2  int   stdio.h:711     # spec:1486 (str ptr)
setvbuf             0x00000C0D  4  int   stdio.h:148     # spec:1419 (ptr str long long)
fflush              0x00000C0E  1  int   stdio.h:119     # spec:1294 (ptr)
_isatty             0x00000C0F  1  int   corecrt_io.h:127  # spec:570 (long)
# _get_osfhandle returns intptr_t -- 64 bits here, fits the single-slot return.
# The value is a real host HANDLE and needs no translation: same address space,
# and handles are opaque scalars on both sides.
_get_osfhandle      0x00000C10  1  int   corecrt_io.h:126  # spec:514 (long)
_fileno             0x00000C11  1  int   stdio.h:80      # spec:444 (ptr)

# index 17: demanded by 335 -- the last name above the cliff.
puts                0x00000C12  1  int   stdio.h:143     # spec:1402 (str)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# indices 18..19: the two msvcrt-only startup entry points.  BOTH ARE FULLY
# CLANG-VERIFIED, including their return kind -- no .spec fallback is used
# anywhere in this file.  That is only true because the header oracle probes
# in msvcrt's CRT mode; getting there needed a tooling fix, recorded here
# because it is exactly the kind of thing that silently regresses:
#
#   Wine's include/msvcrt/ headers describe several C runtimes out of one tree
#   and DEFAULT TO UCRT's (corecrt.h:48 sets _MSVCR_VER to 140, :52 turns that
#   into _UCRT).  Both names below are declared only under `#ifndef _UCRT`
#   (corecrt_startup.h:51 and :53+70), so in the default mode the oracle
#   reported "no declaration found in Wine headers" for them -- a tooling gap
#   presented as an ABI fact -- and vprintf/_vsnprintf resolved to UCRT
#   `static inline` forwarders instead of msvcrt's real import declarations.
#   gen_thunk_pe.py now derives the CRT define from this file's DLL line using
#   Wine's own rule (tools/makedep.c get_crt_define), so msvcrt.dll is probed
#   with -D_MSVCR_VER=0.  See crt_defines_for_dll() in wine_sig.py.
#
#   __getmainargs   corecrt_startup.h:51
#       _ACRTIMP int __cdecl __getmainargs(int *, char ***, char ***, int, int *)
#       Note Wine's own C definition (dlls/msvcrt/data.c:497) spells the last
#       parameter `_startupinfo *` where the header says `int *`.  Harmless
#       here and not worth an upstream patch: _startupinfo is a one-int struct
#       (corecrt_startup.h), and either spelling is one pointer slot.  Cited to
#       the header because the header is the source of record; msvcrt.spec:217
#       `@ cdecl __getmainargs(ptr ptr ptr long ptr)` corroborates the arity.
#
#   __set_app_type  corecrt_startup.h:70
#       Declared under a macro rename: corecrt_startup.h:53 does
#       `#define _set_app_type __set_app_type`, and :70 then declares
#       `_ACRTIMP void __cdecl _set_app_type(_crt_app_type)`, which after
#       preprocessing IS the declaration of __set_app_type.  Line 70 is the
#       expansion location clang reports, so that is what is cited.
#       check_thunk_pe.py's independent regex scanner had to learn to follow
#       object-like `#define ALIAS REAL` renames to see this; before that it
#       reported a name Wine does declare as undeclared.  _crt_app_type is a
#       plain enum, so it is integer class and passes in a register.
#       msvcrt.spec:284 `@ cdecl __set_app_type(long)` corroborates the arity,
#       and dlls/msvcrt/data.c:587 `void CDECL __set_app_type(int)` the void.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__set_app_type      0x00000C13  1  void  corecrt_startup.h:70
__getmainargs       0x00000C14  5  int   corecrt_startup.h:51

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# indices 20..21: the first true `...` variadics.  APPENDED, not inserted --
# indices 0..19 above are ABI and a live dispatcher depends on them.
# Read the variadic banner at the top of this file before touching either line.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# printf -- demanded by 337 of the 339 msvcrt-importing corpus binaries, the
# single largest blocker there is.  stdio.h:701 is the real msvcrt import
# declaration, `_ACRTIMP int __cdecl printf(const char*,...)`, in the
# `#else /* _UCRT && !_NO_CRT_STDIO_INLINE */` branch -- which is the branch
# that applies because the oracle probes this file in msvcrt's own CRT mode
# (-D_MSVCR_VER=0; see the banner at indices 18..19).  stdio.h:421 also spells
# a `static inline printf` for the UCRT branch; that one is not this DLL's
# export.  Pairs with vprintf, which is index 11 above: 1 fixed arg + va_list
# == vprintf's 2 arguments, checked by the oracle, not asserted here.
printf              0x00000C15  1  int   stdio.h:701     variadic=vprintf  # spec:1398 (str)
#
# _snprintf -- the verifiable one: it writes the formatted bytes into a caller
# buffer, so a test can read them back instead of trusting stdout.  3 fixed
# args (char*, size_t, const char*) + va_list == _vsnprintf's 4, and _vsnprintf
# is index 3 above.
#
# FLAGGED, deliberately not hidden: in msvcrt CRT mode the only declaration of
# _snprintf clang can see is the `static inline` forwarder at stdio.h:778 (it
# calls _vsnprintf, which is exactly the pairing asserted here).  The
# _ACRTIMP import declaration at stdio.h:817 is behind `_NO_CRT_STDIO_INLINE`,
# which this build does not set, so it is not the visible one.  Both spell the
# identical prototype, `int __cdecl _snprintf(char*,size_t,const char*,...)`,
# and dlls/msvcrt/msvcrt.spec:917 `@ varargs _snprintf(ptr long str)`
# corroborates the three fixed arguments independently.  msvcrt.dll really does
# export _snprintf regardless of which declaration the header hands a caller.
_snprintf           0x00000C16  3  int   stdio.h:778     variadic=_vsnprintf  # spec:917 (ptr long str)

# _initterm walks a table of the CALLER's initializers.  For a guest caller
# those entries are x86-64 code and this walker is ppc64, so the guest import
# is redirected to a form that wraps each entry as it is reached.  A callback
# mask cannot express this: the argument is a pointer to an ARRAY of function
# pointers, not a function pointer.
GUEST-IMPL   _initterm                        __wine_guest__initterm
GUEST-IMPL   _initterm_e                      __wine_guest__initterm_e

# _lock/_unlock are exported by this module but declared only in its private
# mtdll.h, so the oracle refused them as "no declaration in any Wine header"
# and a game's SDL2.dll got a sentinel for them at PROCESS_ATTACH.
# PROBE-EXTRA appends to the oracle's default translation unit rather than
# replacing it, which is what a module needs when its surface is in Wine's
# public headers AND in a private header of its own.
INCLUDE-DIR   .
PROBE-EXTRA   mtdll.h
PROBE-EXTRA   msvcrt.h        # _amsg_exit, __dllonexit

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-29, guest-cxx-eh-plan Session A follow-up (adversarial review):
# the same __C_specific_handler identity bug and ExceptionObject landmine
# vcruntime140/ucrtbase carried, also live here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# msvcrt.spec:177 declares __C_specific_handler (winnt.h-visible, so FROM-
# SPEC auto emitted a plain TRAP stub, MEASURED at RVA 0xb040 in the built
# msvcrt.dll) -- the exact identity-defeating shape the 2026-08-29 triage
# fixed for vcruntime140/ucrtbase: dlls/ntdll/signal_ppc64.c's frame walk
# recognises __C_specific_handler by ADDRESS IDENTITY against the guest
# ntdll's own export, so any /MD or /MT title whose .xdata handler resolves
# through THIS module's own trap (any VS2010-era-or-earlier /MT title
# statically linking msvcrt's own EH support) would be entered as unknown
# guest code and trap into native ppc64 with an AMD64 DISPATCHER_CONTEXT it
# cannot interpret.  Same fix, same target: forward straight to ntdll's own
# copy, landing on the one address the identity check recognises.
FORWARD __C_specific_handler ntdll.__C_specific_handler

# msvcrt.spec:189-191 also declares __CxxRegisterExceptionObject/
# __CxxUnregisterExceptionObject/__DestructExceptionObject, and msvcrt.h:
# 156-158 declares them too (PROBE-EXTRA msvcrt.h, above) -- so this module
# carried the identical live-trap landmine ucrtbase/vcruntime140 did, MEASURED
# at RVA 0xb050/0xb060/0xb070.  __DestructExceptionObject calls the thrown
# object's own destructor, a GUEST function pointer, so a native trap means
# native ppc64 code indirect-calling x86-64 the moment any FH3/FH4
# personality reaches it -- see ucrtbase.thunks' matching correction for the
# full reasoning.  EXCLUDE makes the refusal real and diagnosable instead of
# an accidental callable stub nobody meant to serve.
EXCLUDE __CxxRegisterExceptionObject
EXCLUDE __CxxUnregisterExceptionObject
EXCLUDE __DestructExceptionObject
